    title: `Neuroplasticity in Adult Language Learning`,
    text: `For decades, a dominant hypothesis in neurolinguistics held that language acquisition becomes increasingly difficult after puberty – the so-called 'critical period hypothesis'. This view, rooted in Lenneberg's 1967 work, suggested that the brain's lateralisation of language functions to the left hemisphere solidifies by adolescence, reducing neural plasticity. However, recent advances in functional neuroimaging have challenged a strict interpretation of this model, revealing that adult brains retain remarkable capacity for structural and functional reorganisation.

Neuroplasticity – the brain's ability to form new synaptic connections – persists throughout life. In language learning, adult brains rely more heavily on the right hemisphere and prefrontal cortex compared to children, who predominantly use left perisylvian regions. A 2020 longitudinal study by Mårtensson et al. tracked military interpreters undergoing intensive language training. After eight months, participants showed increased grey matter density in the hippocampus and superior temporal gyrus, alongside strengthened white matter tracts in the arcuate fasciculus. Crucially, these changes correlated with proficiency gains, not with age.

Nevertheless, adults face distinct challenges. Implicit learning – absorbing grammatical patterns without conscious effort – becomes less automatic. Adults tend to rely on explicit, declarative memory systems mediated by the hippocampus, whereas children leverage procedural memory involving basal ganglia. This shift explains why adults excel at vocabulary acquisition but struggle with accent and morphosyntax. Research by Ullman (2015) proposes the 'declarative/procedural model', noting that while procedural learning declines, declarative memory remains robust, allowing adults to compensate through meta-linguistic awareness.

Practical applications abound. Immersion-based methods that mimic childlike implicit learning, such as 'processing instruction' and 'task-based language teaching', have shown efficacy in adult classrooms. Moreover, activities promoting cross-modal integration – listening while simultaneously reading – enhance neural binding. Sleep also plays a role: consolidation of novel words and grammatical rules occurs during slow-wave sleep, and adults who nap after learning sessions retain vocabulary up to 30% better.

In conclusion, while adults may not achieve native-like phonology without exceptional effort, their neuroplasticity is far from static. The brain's lifelong ability to remap itself means that with optimal conditions – sufficient input, low stress, and retrieval practice – significant language gains are attainable at any age. This reframing has profound implications for second-language education policy and for combating age-related cognitive decline.`,
