    id: "passage_008",
    tag: "Behavioural Economics",
    level: "Academic",
    title: `Behavioural Economics of Retirement Savings`,
    text: `For decades, neoclassical economics assumed that individuals make rational, forward-looking decisions about retirement savings, optimising lifetime utility. However, the explosion of behavioural economics – pioneered by Kahneman, Tversky, and Thaler – has revealed systematic biases that derail even well-intentioned savers. The 'retirement savings puzzle' refers to the widespread failure to save adequately despite clear financial incentives and employer matches.

Present bias – the tendency to overweight immediate gratification at the expense of future wellbeing – is the primary culprit. Hyperbolic discounting models show that individuals demand exponentially higher returns to delay consumption. When asked whether they would prefer $100 today or $110 in a month, most choose today. Yet the same person prefers $110 in 12 months over $100 in 11 months, revealing time-inconsistency. This explains why employees delay increasing 401(k) contributions even when employers offer dollar-for-dollar matches up to 5% of salary – an immediate 100% return on investment.

Loss aversion, another key bias, causes people to treat potential losses as twice as painful as equivalent gains. In the savings context, contributions feel like 'losses' of disposable income, whereas foregone matching feels like hypothetical gain. A 2019 study by Beshears et al. found that framing the match as 'free money you will lose if you don't contribute' increases participation by 17 percentage points compared to standard framing.

Default effects represent one of the most powerful nudges. Madrian and Shea's landmark 2001 study at a US corporation showed that automatic enrolment (opt-out) increased 401(k) participation from 37% to 86% within three years. Even more strikingly, 'auto-escalation' – automatically increasing contribution rates annually – raised average savings rates from 3.5% to 11.2% without active employee decisions. Critics argue this paternalism infringes autonomy, but Thaler's 'libertarian paternalism' defends defaults as choice-preserving.

Other interventions include 'save more tomorrow' (SMarT) programmes, where employees commit to future increases tied to pay raises, leveraging present bias against itself. A Chilean reform automatically enrolled all formal workers into a default fund with opt-out, raising coverage from 50% to 85%. Additionally, simplification of enrolment forms (reducing from 15 to 4 pages) increased participation by 10% in a US field experiment.

Despite successes, behavioural interventions cannot compensate for inadequate wages or regressive tax policies. The optimal solution combines automatic mechanisms with financial literacy and structural reforms. As Thaler noted, 'To help people save more, we must accept that humans are not Econs.'`,
