    title: `The Political Economy of Water Scarcity`,
    text: `Water scarcity affects over 2 billion people globally, and climate change is intensifying the crisis. Unlike fossil fuels, water has no substitutes, yet it is systematically undervalued. The 'water-energy-food nexus' framework recognises that these resources are interdependent: energy production requires water (thermoelectric cooling, hydropower), water treatment requires energy, and food production consumes 70% of global freshwater withdrawals.

Groundwater depletion is the most urgent issue. The world's largest aquifers – the Ogallala (US), Guarani (South America), and North China Plain – are being mined at rates exceeding natural recharge. A 2020 study by the Water in the West programme used GRACE satellite data to measure groundwater storage changes. The Ogallala Aquifer lost 270 km³ (comparable to Lake Erie) between 2000-2015, with depletion rates of 10 cm/year in intensively irrigated areas. At current rates, 30% of the aquifer will be unusable for agriculture by 2050.

Institutional responses vary. Australia's Murray-Darling Basin Plan (2012) established a cap on extractions and created water markets allowing trade of entitlements. A 2021 evaluation found that water markets reduced agricultural impact during drought by 40% compared to administrative allocation. However, markets concentrate ownership: 10% of irrigators control 60% of water rights. Chile's Water Code (1981, amended 2005) fully privatised water, leading to hoarding by mining companies and protests in the Petorca Valley.

Transboundary water conflicts risk militarisation. The Nile (Ethiopia's Grand Ethiopian Renaissance Dam, GERD), Tigris-Euphrates (Turkey's dams), and Indus (India-Pakistan) are flashpoints. A 2019 study by the Pacific Institute recorded 300 water-related conflicts since 2000, mostly in the Middle East and South Asia. The UN Watercourses Convention (1997) provides a framework for equitable use, but only 36 countries have ratified.

Technological solutions include desalination (supplying 1% of global drinking water; energy-intensive, producing brine as waste), wastewater recycling (Singapore's NEWater supplies 40% of demand), and drip irrigation (50% water savings). Solar-powered desalination has dropped in cost from $5/m³ (2000) to $1/m³ (2023). However, rebound effects – cheaper water encourages overuse – must be managed.

Future governance requires pricing water to reflect full social cost (including ecosystem damage), integrated resource management, and demand-side measures (e.g., drought-resistant crops). As water economist Mariana Mazzucato notes, 'Water is not a commodity like any other. It requires public stewardship, not just market mechanisms.'`,
