    title: `The Biophysics of Sperm Competition`,
    text: `Sperm competition – competition between sperm from different males to fertilise the same egg – is a powerful evolutionary force. Across the animal kingdom, it has shaped testis size, sperm morphology, and ejaculate composition. The 'raffle principle' predicts that males increase fertilisation success by ejaculating more sperm when rivals are likely present. A 2017 meta-analysis of 167 species found that relative testis mass (controlling for body size) increased by 300% in species with high sperm competition risk, from primates to insects.

Postcopulatory sexual selection also drives sperm trait divergence. In species where females mate multiply, sperm with longer tails (flagella) swim faster, gaining fertilisation advantage. A 2019 study of 30 mammalian species by the University of Sheffield showed that sperm tail length correlates with swimming velocity (r=0.72) and that species with high competition have 40% longer sperm tails. However, longer tails require more energy; there is a trade-off between speed and longevity.

Sperm polymorphism – simultaneous production of normal 'fertilising' sperm and non-fertile 'parasperm' – occurs in some insects and molluscs. Parasperm may block rival sperm, provide nutrients, or act as kamikaze units. In Drosophila pseudoobscura, males produce 30% parasperm when reared with rivals, reducing the fertilisation success of subsequent males by 60%. A 2021 paper by Lüpold et al. identified a genetic locus controlling parasperm production, under positive selection in promiscuous species.

Ejaculate order matters. Last-male precedence (the last male to mate sires most offspring) is common in insects (e.g., crickets) due to mechanical displacement of rival sperm. Females can also bias sperm use cryptically. In birds, females eject sperm from less-preferred males after copulation. A 2018 experiment with zebra finches found that females mated to males with superior songs retained 50% more of their sperm, biasing paternity irrespective of insemination order.

Human sperm competition evidence is indirect. Average human testis size (relative to body mass) is intermediate between monogamous (gibbons) and promiscuous (chimpanzees) primates, suggesting moderate competition historically. A 2020 study of 5,000 men found that those who spent more time away from their partners (e.g., business travel) had 27% higher sperm concentrations upon return – consistent with the raffle principle. Additionally, men's sperm quality decreases after extended cohabitation (the 'Coolidge effect' reversed).

Applications include livestock breeding (selecting for high-competition sperm traits), conservation biology (assessing inbreeding depression), and understanding recurrent pregnancy loss. As reproductive biologist Tim Birkhead concludes, 'Sperm are not just carriers of genes; they are sophisticated agents shaped by a hidden battlefield.'`,
