    level: "Academic",
    title: `The Geochemistry of Ocean Acidification`,
    text: `Ocean acidification (OA) – the decrease in seawater pH caused by absorption of anthropogenic CO2 – has increased ocean acidity by 30% since the Industrial Revolution (pH from 8.2 to 8.05). The chemical reaction CO2 + H2O → H2CO3 → HCO3- + H+ consumes carbonate ions (CO3 2-), which are essential for calcifying organisms (corals, molluscs, plankton). A 2020 IPCC report projected that under RCP8.5, surface ocean pH will drop to 7.7 by 2100 – a 150% acidity increase.

Aragonite saturation state (Ωarag) predicts calcification. Ωarag = [Ca2+][CO3 2-]/Ksp. When Ωarag <1, aragonite (coral skeleton mineral) dissolves. The Southern Ocean and Arctic are already undersaturated in winter for pteropods (sea butterflies), whose shells dissolve within 45 days in pH 7.8 seawater. A 2019 study by Bednaršek et al. found that 53% of pteropods in the Southern Ocean had severe shell dissolution, up from 24% in 2008.

Physiological impacts extend beyond calcification. Elevated pCO2 causes hypercapnia (CO2 poisoning) in fish, impairing olfactory function. Clownfish larvae exposed to OA lose ability to detect predator cues, increasing mortality by 70% in experimental tanks. A 2018 meta-analysis of 200 OA studies found that calcification decreases by 35% on average, but some species (crustaceans) show increased calcification at moderate pCO2 due to acid-base compensation mechanisms.

Ecological tipping points are likely. Coral reef dissolution exceeds accretion when Ωarag <2.5. Currently, 30% of reefs are below this threshold. A 2021 study of Australia's Great Barrier Reef projected that by 2050, 80% of reefs will be net erosional (dissolving faster than growing). This will collapse reef habitat, affecting 500 million people dependent on reef fisheries.

Geoengineering proposals include ocean alkalinity enhancement (OAE) – adding crushed olivine or limestone to seawater to neutralise acid. A 2020 modelling study showed that spreading 1 billion tonnes of olivine per year could restore pH to pre-industrial levels by 2100 but requires 10x current mining output. Biological carbon capture (kelp farming) has co-benefits but limited scale.

Mitigation requires emissions reduction. OA cannot be reversed locally; it is a global CO2 problem. As marine chemist Scott Doney notes, 'Ocean acidification is the hidden twin of climate change – less visible but equally devastating.'`,
