    id: "passage_057",
    tag: "Science",
    level: "Academic",
    title: `The Physics of Ball Lightning`,
    text: `Ball lightning – luminous, spherical electrical phenomena lasting seconds – has been reported for centuries but remained unexplained. Unlike conventional lightning, ball lightning floats, moves erratically, and sometimes enters buildings through closed windows. A 2018 global survey compiled 5,000 eyewitness accounts; common features: diameter 10-50 cm, duration 1-5 seconds, colours white/yellow/orange, associated with thunderstorms, and occasional hissing sound. 3% reported explosion upon disappearance.

The vapourised silicon hypothesis (2000) proposes that lightning strikes soil, vapourising silicon dioxide (silica), which condenses into a glowing aerosol. Laboratory experiments by the Russian Academy of Sciences in 2012 replicated ball lightning by discharging 10,000 volts into a silicon wafer submerged in liquid nitrogen – producing floating luminous balls lasting 3-8 seconds. Spectroscopic analysis confirmed silicon vapour recombination.

The microwave cavity theory suggests that lightning generates surface plasmons on a plasma bubble confined by microwave radiation. A 2017 paper by Dikhtyar and Jerby used a 2.45 GHz microwave source to create plasma balls in a waveguide; the balls persisted for 0.5 seconds after power cutoff. However, microwave cavities require precise conditions rarely met in nature.

The triboelectric model attributes ball lightning to charge separation during thunderstorms, forming a charged aerosol that self-confines via Coulomb repulsion. A 2020 numerical simulation by the University of Innsbruck reproduced observed lifetimes and trajectories, but the model fails to explain entry through small openings (charge shielding limits this).

Ball lightning's rarity (estimated 1 in 10,000 lightning strikes) impedes study. The only natural ball lightning ever spectroscopically recorded was by Chinese scientists in 2014, when a lightning strike 900 metres away produced a ball that moved 40 metres over 1.6 seconds. Emission lines of silicon, iron, and calcium were detected – supporting the vapourised soil hypothesis.

Practical applications include plasma containment (fusion research) and ball lightning generators for industrial plasma processing. As physicist John Abrahamson noted, 'Ball lightning is not supernatural; it is a transient state of matter we are only beginning to engineer.'`,
