      { id: 6, type: "mcq", q: `The vapourised silicon hypothesis proposes that ball lightning forms when lightning:`, opts: [`A) strikes soil and vapourises silica into a glowing aerosol`, `B) is confined by microwaves`, `C) separates charge in clouds`, `D) strikes open water`], answer: `A`, explanation: `Lightning 'strikes soil, vapourising silicon dioxide (silica), which condenses into a glowing aerosol'.` },
      { id: 7, type: "mcq", q: `The 2014 Chinese spectroscopic recording detected emission lines of:`, opts: [`A) only oxygen`, `B) hydrogen and helium`, `C) carbon and nitrogen`, `D) silicon, iron, and calcium`], answer: `D`, explanation: `'Emission lines of silicon, iron, and calcium were detected'.` },
      { id: 8, type: "mcq", q: `The microwave cavity theory was tested by Dikhtyar and Jerby using a:`, opts: [`A) silicon wafer in liquid nitrogen`, `B) 2.45 GHz microwave source`, `C) lightning rod`, `D) Coulomb simulation`], answer: `B`, explanation: `They 'used a 2.45 GHz microwave source to create plasma balls in a waveguide'.` },
      { id: 9, type: "mcq", q: `Ball lightning is estimated to occur in roughly:`, opts: [`A) every lightning strike`, `B) 1 in 100 strikes`, `C) 1 in 10,000 lightning strikes`, `D) 1 in a million strikes`], answer: `C`, explanation: `Its rarity is 'estimated 1 in 10,000 lightning strikes'.` },
      { id: 10, type: "sentence_completion", q: `Complete the sentence.`, sentenceTemplate: `The 2018 survey found that ball lightning typically lasts ___ seconds.`, answer: `1-5`, acceptedAnswers: [`1 to 5`, `one to five`], explanation: `Common features include 'duration 1-5 seconds'.` },
      { id: 11, type: "sentence_completion", q: `Complete the sentence.`, sentenceTemplate: `The ___ model attributes ball lightning to charge separation during thunderstorms.`, answer: `triboelectric`, explanation: `'The triboelectric model attributes ball lightning to charge separation during thunderstorms'.` },
      { id: 12, type: "short_answer", q: `What element, vapourised from soil, is central to the leading hypothesis of ball lightning?`, answer: `silicon`, acceptedAnswers: [`silica`], explanation: `The 'vapourised silicon hypothesis' attributes ball lightning to vapourised silica.` },
      { id: 13, type: "short_answer", q: `In the 2012 experiment, how many volts were discharged into the silicon wafer?`, answer: `10,000 volts`, acceptedAnswers: [`10,000`, `10000`], explanation: `Researchers discharged '10,000 volts into a silicon wafer'.` }
