    title: `The Biogeography of Island Evolution`,
    text: `Islands are natural laboratories of evolution. Their isolation, limited area, and unique species assemblages produce evolutionary trajectories distinct from continents. The Theory of Island Biogeography (MacArthur & Wilson, 1967) predicts species richness as equilibrium between immigration and extinction: S = c * A^z, where A is area, z is slope (0.2-0.35). A 2018 meta-analysis of 1,000 islands confirmed the model, explaining 70% of variance in species richness.

Gigantism and dwarfism are common. On islands without predators, small mammals become larger (gigantism: Mallorcan giant dormouse, 25x mainland size); large mammals become smaller (dwarfism: Mediterranean dwarf elephants, 90% smaller). A 2020 genomic study of Sicilian dwarf elephants found accelerated evolution in growth-regulating genes (IGF1, GHR). The 'island rule' – body size shifts towards intermediate optimum – has been debated; a 2021 Bayesian meta-analysis supported the rule for mammals, not birds.

Endemism rates are extreme. On Madagascar, 90% of plants and 85% of animals are endemic (found nowhere else). Lemurs diversified from a single colonist 60 million years ago into 110 species. A 2019 phylogenetic study estimated that lemur extinction risk (94% threatened) is 5x higher than primates globally, due to habitat loss and hunting. Similarly, New Zealand's endemic fauna (kiwi, kakapo) evolved without mammalian predators, leaving them vulnerable to introduced rats and stoats.

Adaptive radiation produces phenotypic diversity from a common ancestor. Darwin's finches (Galápagos) evolved 18 species with different beak shapes adapted to food sources. A 40-year longitudinal study by Grant & Grant (1973-2018) documented natural selection in real time: drought in 1977 killed 85% of finches, favouring larger beaks (2 mm increase); a 1983 El Niño reversed selection. Whole-genome sequencing identified the ALX1 gene controlling beak shape.

Human colonisation causes extinction. Pacific islands lost 1,500 bird species after Polynesian settlement (Lombard, 2023). The moa (giant flightless bird of New Zealand) was driven extinct by Maori hunting within 200 years of arrival. Invasive species remain the primary threat: brown tree snakes eliminated 10 of 12 native forest birds on Guam.

Conservation strategies include eradication of invasives (rats from Macquarie Island), captive breeding (kakapo), and island restoration. As evolutionary biologist Jonathan Losos notes, 'Islands are not just remote outposts; they are windows into the deep rules of evolution.'`,
