    id: "passage_063",
    tag: "Economics",
    level: "Academic",
    title: `The Monetary Economics of Cryptocurrency`,
    text: `Cryptocurrencies – decentralised digital assets using blockchain technology – have grown from Bitcoin's 2009 launch to over 10,000 currencies with $1.2 trillion market capitalisation (2024). Unlike fiat money (government-issued), cryptocurrencies lack central authority, relying on cryptographic proof and distributed consensus. The 'blockchain trilemma' (Buterin) states that decentralisation, security, and scalability cannot all be maximised simultaneously.

Bitcoin uses proof-of-work (PoW) consensus, where miners compete to solve hash puzzles. Energy consumption is immense: Bitcoin network uses 120 TWh annually (0.3% of global electricity, comparable to Argentina). A 2022 Cambridge study found that 40% of Bitcoin mining uses renewable energy (hydro, wind). China's 2021 mining ban shifted 50% of hash power to the US and Kazakhstan. Each Bitcoin transaction uses 1,200 kWh (powering 40 US homes for one day).

Ethereum introduced smart contracts – self-executing code on blockchain. The 2022 'Merge' transitioned Ethereum from PoW to proof-of-stake (PoS), reducing energy use by 99.9%. PoS validators stake cryptocurrency as collateral, earning transaction fees. A 2023 analysis found that PoS reduces decentralisation risks (wealthy validators dominate). Over 4,000 decentralised applications (DeFi, NFTs, games) run on Ethereum.

Stablecoins (USDC, USDT) are pegged to fiat currency to reduce volatility. They maintain reserves (cash, treasuries) in banks; a 2021 study found that 50% of USDT was backed by commercial paper (risky assets). The 2023 USDC depeg (dropping to $0.87) during Silicon Valley Bank collapse shook confidence. Regulation is emerging: EU's Markets in Crypto-Assets (MiCA) Regulation (2024) requires stablecoin issuers to hold 1:1 reserves and register with authorities.

Central Bank Digital Currencies (CBDCs) differ from cryptocurrencies: they are state-issued, centralised, and legal tender. China's e-CNY has 260 million users (2023); Nigeria's eNaira has 5 million. A 2024 IMF survey found 110 countries (95% of global GDP) exploring CBDCs. Privacy concerns arise: a digital yuan allows real-time tracking; Bahamas' Sand Dollar requires KYC for all transactions.

Cryptocurrency's future includes interoperability (cross-chain bridges), zero-knowledge proofs (ZK-rollups for privacy), and institutional adoption (BlackRock Bitcoin ETF). As economist Nouriel Roubini warns, 'Most crypto is speculative garbage, but blockchain may have genuine utility for settlement and provenance.'`,
