    title: `The Quantum Biology of Photosynthesis`,
    text: `Photosynthesis converts sunlight to chemical energy with near-unity quantum efficiency. The light-harvesting complexes (LHCs) contain chlorophyll molecules arranged in antenna arrays that absorb photons and transfer energy to reaction centres. A 2018 ultrafast spectroscopy study found that energy transfer occurs on femtosecond to picosecond timescales, too fast for classical hopping; quantum coherence (wave-like delocalisation) enables simultaneous sampling of multiple pathways.

Exciton coherence was first observed in green sulphur bacteria (Chlorobium tepidum) at 77K. A 2010 experiment at room temperature showed coherence persisting for 300 femtoseconds, long enough for energy transfer. The Fenna-Matthews-Olson (FMO) protein complex has been extensively studied: 7 bacteriochlorophyll molecules arranged so that exciton wavefunctions overlap, creating quantum beats in 2D electronic spectra. A 2020 theoretical model found that environmental noise (phonons) actually enhances transport (environment-assisted quantum transport, ENAQT).

The quantum efficiency puzzle: why does photosynthesis not degrade coherence faster? The answer may lie in 'vibrational assistance'. Protein scaffolding creates specific vibrations that couple to electronic transitions, creating polaritons (hybrid light-matter states). A 2021 experiment using cryo-electron microscopy resolved the structure of plant PSII (Photosystem II) at 2.3Å, revealing that pigment-protein interactions tune electronic energy levels for maximum overlap.

Charge separation at reaction centres occurs via quantum tunnelling. In purple bacteria, the special pair (P870) donates an electron to bacteriopheophytin in 3 picoseconds, with zero activation energy. A 2019 kinetic isotope experiment (deuterated solvents) confirmed tunnelling by lack of temperature dependence. The electron then moves to quinone via sequential tunnelling steps, with overall quantum yield of 0.98.

Spin dynamics affect radical pair recombination. The carotenoid triplet state (formed via intersystem crossing) protects chlorophyll from photodamage. A 2022 magnetic field effect study found that applying 50 mT field increased recombination yield by 15%, consistent with singlet-triplet mixing theory. The avian magnetic compass may use similar radical pair mechanism (cryptochromes in retina).

Implications for artificial photosynthesis include quantum dot solar cells (using CdSe quantum dots to generate multiple excitons), organic photovoltaics (singlet fission increasing efficiency beyond Shockley-Queisser limit), and light-harvesting antennas for biohybrid systems. As quantum biologist Gregory Scholes noted, 'Nature discovered quantum optimisation billions of years before quantum computers were imagined.'`,
