    id: "passage_071",
    tag: "Sociology",
    level: "Academic",
    title: `The Epidemiology of Food Insecurity`,
    text: `Food insecurity – limited or uncertain access to adequate food – affects 2.4 billion people globally (30% of population). The Food and Agriculture Organization (FAO) defines four levels: food secure, mildly insecure (worry about running out), moderately insecure (reduced quality/variety), and severely insecure (skipping meals, going hungry). A 2021 global assessment found that 800 million people experienced severe food insecurity, with Africa (60%) and South Asia (35%) highest.

The nutrition transition paradox: obesity coexists with food insecurity. In high-income countries, food-insecure households have 30% higher obesity rates. Cheap, energy-dense, nutrient-poor foods (ultra-processed) are affordable and shelf-stable. A 2019 study of 50,000 US households found that food-insecure children consumed 200 more calories daily from snacks and sugary drinks, yet had lower intakes of fruits, vegetables, and protein. 'Food swamps' (neighbourhoods with 4x more fast food than grocery stores) predict obesity better than food deserts (grocery absence).

Social determinants explain disparities. A 2020 longitudinal study of 10,000 Canadian families found that job loss raised food insecurity risk 500% within 3 months. Single mothers have 3x higher rates than two-parent households. Disability, housing instability, and medical debt are additional drivers. The COVID-19 pandemic doubled food insecurity in the US (from 11% to 23%) within one month, with Black and Hispanic households 2-3x more affected.

Health consequences are severe. Food-insecure adults have 40% higher rates of diabetes, 50% higher hypertension, and 2x higher depression. A 2021 meta-analysis of 100 studies found that food insecurity increases all-cause mortality by 30% after controlling for income. For children, effects include developmental delay (60% higher risk), hospitalisation (2x), and poor school performance (grade repetition 40% higher).

Interventions include food assistance programmes (SNAP in US, food banks, school meals). A 2018 randomised trial of summer meal programmes for 5,000 children reduced food insecurity by 50% and improved weight status. Universal free school meals (UK, Finland) reduce stigma and improve attendance. Cash transfers (e.g., Brazil's Bolsa Família) reduced moderate food insecurity by 25% in 10 years.

Structural solutions require poverty reduction. A 2022 simulation estimated that raising US minimum wage to $15 would reduce food insecurity by 30%, at $40 billion annual cost – cheaper than obesity-related healthcare ($147 billion). As nutrition economist Will Masters concludes, 'Food insecurity is not a scarcity problem; global food production exceeds demand. It is a distribution problem rooted in poverty and policy failure.'`,
