    title: `The Philosophy of Animal Rights`,
    text: `Animal rights philosophy challenges the assumption that non-human animals lack moral standing. Peter Singer's 'Animal Liberation' (1975) applied utilitarian calculus: sentient beings (capable of suffering) deserve equal consideration of interests. Speciesism – discrimination based on species – is analogous to racism or sexism. A 2020 survey of 1,000 philosophy professors found that 70% agreed with Singer's principle (equal consideration for animals), but only 30% were vegan.

Tom Regan's rights view (1983) argues that animals (mammals, birds, some others) are 'subjects-of-a-life' with inherent value, not just sentient. They have rights not to be harmed (negative rights) but not rights to assistance. A 2018 analysis of Regan's criteria (preferences, memory, pleasure/pain) applied to 500 animal species found that 90% of mammals, 70% of birds, 40% of reptiles meet threshold. Invertebrates (bees, octopus) are debated – octopus passed mirror test (self-awareness) in 2021.

The marginal case argument: if humans with severe cognitive disabilities (infants, dementia patients) have moral status despite lacking rationality, then animals with equal cognitive capacity also qualify. A 2019 study found that 80% of respondents agreed that severely disabled humans have moral status; 60% agreed that chimpanzees (similar cognitive level) also have moral status. The 'argument from species normality' (humans have potential for rationality) is rebutted by potentiality critiques.

Legal personhood for animals is emerging. India's 2019 ruling declared all animals 'legal persons' with rights (freedom from cruelty, right to habitat). Colombia's 2020 habeas corpus for a bear (Chucho) initially granted, then reversed. A 2021 global survey found that 10 countries have recognised great ape rights (non-human persons); 5 countries banned factory farming (Switzerland, Norway). The EU's 2022 animal welfare directive recognises sentience (article 13 TFEU).

Factory farming practices (confinement, mutilation, slaughter) cause immense suffering. A 2020 review of 1,000 farms found that 70% of laying hens kept in battery cages (<0.05 m² per bird); 90% of pigs and calves in crates preventing movement. Cage-free egg production costs 30% more; EU banned battery cages in 2012 (20% of farms converted). Meat consumption continues rising (360 million tonnes/year 2020, projected 500 million tonnes 2050).

Alternatives: cultivated meat (cell culture) and plant-based substitutes (Beyond Meat) reduce animal suffering. A 2022 life-cycle assessment found that cultivated meat reduces land use by 80% and water by 90% but currently costs 10x conventional meat. As philosopher Martha Nussbaum argues, 'Justice for animals requires not just preventing cruelty but enabling flourishing according to their species' capacities.'`,
