    text: `Dark matter comprises 85% of matter in the universe, yet its nature remains unknown. Evidence: galaxy rotation curves (Vera Rubin, 1970s) showed flat rotation velocities (v≈constant) beyond visible matter, requiring additional mass. A 2019 analysis of 200 spiral galaxies found that the Tully-Fisher relation (v^4 ∝ luminosity) matches dark matter halo predictions. Gravitational lensing (distortion of background galaxies) maps dark matter distribution.

Dark matter candidates: WIMPs (Weakly Interacting Massive Particles, 10-1000 GeV) are favoured by supersymmetry (neutralino). Direct detection experiments (LUX, XENON1T) have null results, excluding WIMP cross-sections >10^-46 cm² (100x lower than 2000 limits). A 2022 report from LZ (LUX-ZEPLIN) set new limits (10^-48 cm² for 30 GeV WIMPs), ruling out remaining parameter space for canonical SUSY models.

Axions (10^-6 - 10^-3 eV) are alternative candidates proposed to solve strong-CP problem. Axion haloscopes (ADMX, HAYSTAC) search for axion-photon conversion in magnetic cavities. A 2021 detection claim (2σ) at 3.5 μeV not yet replicated. A 2020 cosmological analysis found that axion-like particles could explain small-scale structure missing in WIMP simulations (cusp-core problem). Axions also could be dark matter with mass window 10^-22 - 10^-20 eV (fuzzy dark matter).

Primordial black holes (PBHs) formed in early universe (10^-5 - 10^3 solar masses) could constitute dark matter. Constraints from microlensing (MACHO survey, 1990s) and accretion signatures rule out PBHs as dominant component. However, PBHs of asteroid mass (10^16 - 10^22 g) evade detection and are still viable. A 2021 analysis of LIGO-Virgo black hole mergers (86 events) found no evidence for PBH binaries, but 30% of events could be PBH origin.

Modified gravity theories (MOND, TeVeS) attempt to explain rotation curves without dark matter. MOND (Milgrom, 1983) modifies Newtonian dynamics at acceleration a0 ≈ 1.2×10^-10 m/s². A 2018 test of MOND against galaxy cluster dynamics (Bullet Cluster) failed: MOND cannot explain 2x lensing separation without additional dark matter. The combination of cosmic microwave background (Planck 2018) and large-scale structure (BOSS) strongly favours ΛCDM (cold dark matter + cosmological constant).

Future searches: Cherenkov Telescope Array (gamma rays from WIMP annihilation), LISA (gravitational waves from PBH mergers), and nuclear recoil detectors (superfluid helium). As astrophysicist Katherine Freese concludes, 'Dark matter is not a failure of physics; it is a clue that the Standard Model is incomplete, pointing to new particles and forces.'`,
